{"format":"kards-original-reference","version":1,"id":"d80ccd49ff7af4d8","name":"card_unit_somoa_s35","package":"/Game/Blueprints/Cards/France/units/card_unit_somoa_s35","parent":"UBaseCardObject","card":{"title":"SOMUA S35","text":"The first unit you deploy each turn gets +1+1 and -1 operation cost.","flavorText":"The SOMUA S35 was a French cavalry tank which was used at the beginning of World War II in the Battle of France, and later as a salvaged vehicle in the German and Italian armies, and the Free French Forces during the liberation. In 1940, it was considered the best French tank available, alongside the B1 Bis heavy tank, and it was in service in the French Light Mechanized Divisions. The French cavalry tanks aimed at offering a good compromise between firepower, protection and mobility, to fit the needs of modern cavalry on the battlefield. They were to be used in both anti-personnel and anti-tank roles. Thanks to its speed, its solid armor and its efficient cannon for that time, the SOMUA S35 proved to be a valuable asset on battlefields such as Hannut in Belgium, the first large tank battle of World War II. The thickness and inclination of its armor offered solid protection against shells from Panzer III and Panzer IV tanks, while its own 47mm cannon could in theory pierce their armor. It was also armed with a 7.5mm coaxial machine gun, and was manned by three crew members. However, it suffered from low visibility and the lack of a radio in each vehicle (only the platoon leader had one). And its worst flaw, like many other French tanks, was its “one-man turret” design. The tank commander had to man the gun and reload it, on top of fulfilling its own role (target acquisition, giving instructions, etc.) which seriously hindered the tank’s rate of fire and general efficiency. Nonetheless, the SOMUA S35 allowed the French Light Mechanized Divisions to accomplish their mission and to delay the German advance in May 1940. 430 were built between 1936 and 1940.","faction":"France","type":"tank","rarity":"Rare","cost":3,"operationCost":2,"attack":3,"defense":4},"defaults":{"UberGraphFrame":{},"Name":"card_unit_somoa_s35","title":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_somoa_s35_title","SourceString":"SOMUA S35","LocalizedString":"SOMUA S35"},"Type":"ETypeEnum::tank","attack":3,"defense":4,"range":1,"faction":"EFactionEnum::France","kredits":3,"Text":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_somoa_s35_text","SourceString":"The first unit you deploy each turn gets +1+1 and -1 operation cost.","LocalizedString":"The first unit you deploy each turn gets +1+1 and -1 operation cost."},"flavorText":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_somoa_s35_history","SourceString":"The SOMUA S35 was a French cavalry tank which was used at the beginning of World War II in the Battle of France, and later as a salvaged vehicle in the German and Italian armies, and the Free French Forces during the liberation. In 1940, it was considered the best French tank available, alongside the B1 Bis heavy tank, and it was in service in the French Light Mechanized Divisions. The French cavalry tanks aimed at offering a good compromise between firepower, protection and mobility, to fit the needs of modern cavalry on the battlefield. They were to be used in both anti-personnel and anti-tank roles. Thanks to its speed, its solid armor and its efficient cannon for that time, the SOMUA S35 proved to be a valuable asset on battlefields such as Hannut in Belgium, the first large tank battle of World War II. The thickness and inclination of its armor offered solid protection against shells from Panzer III and Panzer IV tanks, while its own 47mm cannon could in theory pierce their armor. It was also armed with a 7.5mm coaxial machine gun, and was manned by three crew members. However, it suffered from low visibility and the lack of a radio in each vehicle (only the platoon leader had one). And its worst flaw, like many other French tanks, was its “one-man turret” design. The tank commander had to man the gun and reload it, on top of fulfilling its own role (target acquisition, giving instructions, etc.) which seriously hindered the tank’s rate of fire and general efficiency. Nonetheless, the SOMUA S35 allowed the French Light Mechanized Divisions to accomplish their mission and to delay the German advance in May 1940. 430 were built between 1936 and 1940.","LocalizedString":"The SOMUA S35 was a French cavalry tank which was used at the beginning of World War II in the Battle of France, and later as a salvaged vehicle in the German and Italian armies, and the Free French Forces during the liberation. In 1940, it was considered the best French tank available, alongside the B1 Bis heavy tank, and it was in service in the French Light Mechanized Divisions. The French cavalry tanks aimed at offering a good compromise between firepower, protection and mobility, to fit the needs of modern cavalry on the battlefield. They were to be used in both anti-personnel and anti-tank roles. Thanks to its speed, its solid armor and its efficient cannon for that time, the SOMUA S35 proved to be a valuable asset on battlefields such as Hannut in Belgium, the first large tank battle of World War II. The thickness and inclination of its armor offered solid protection against shells from Panzer III and Panzer IV tanks, while its own 47mm cannon could in theory pierce their armor. It was also armed with a 7.5mm coaxial machine gun, and was manned by three crew members. However, it suffered from low visibility and the lack of a radio in each vehicle (only the platoon leader had one). And its worst flaw, like many other French tanks, was its “one-man turret” design. The tank commander had to man the gun and reload it, on top of fulfilling its own role (target acquisition, giving instructions, etc.) which seriously hindered the tank’s rate of fire and general efficiency. Nonetheless, the SOMUA S35 allowed the French Light Mechanized Divisions to accomplish their mission and to delay the German advance in May 1940. 430 were built between 1936 and 1940."},"operationCost":2,"rarity":"ERarityEnum::Rare","cardSet":"ECardSetEnum::BloodAndIron","EffectType":"ECardEffectTypes::Trigger","usedTriggers":["ERegisteredCardFunction::OnOtherCardPlayedFromHand","ERegisteredCardFunction::OnEndOfTurn"],"heavyArmor":1,"Image":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/Images/France/Mini_Ally_Expansion_25/somua_s35.somua_s35","SubPathString":""},"artist":"David Pentland","historyPhoto":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Assets/Textures/HistoricalPhotos/SOMUA_S35.SOMUA_S35","SubPathString":""},"historyPhotoSize":{"X":512.0,"Y":341.0},"historyPhotoDescription":{"Namespace":"card_france","Key":"card_unit_somoa_s35_photo","SourceString":"SOMUA S35 tanks and their crews, 1940.","LocalizedString":"SOMUA S35 tanks and their crews, 1940."},"blowUpExplosionNiagara":{"ObjectName":"NiagaraSystem'NS_ExplosionArmourM'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/Effects/Niagara/NS_ExplosionArmourM.36"},"blowUpCameraShake":{"ObjectName":"BlueprintGeneratedClass'CameraShakeHeavy_C'","ObjectPath":"kards/Content/examples/GrenadePack/Blueprints/CameraShakeHeavy.0"},"OnAttackVisualsSoft":{"AssetPathName":"/Game/Blueprints/Effects/Tanks/BP_AttackTank.BP_AttackTank_C","SubPathString":""},"threat_level":0.1},"variables":[{"name":"tempCard","type":"UBaseCardObject*","direction":"in"},{"name":"otherUnitPlayed","type":"bool","direction":"in"},{"name":"buffedCard","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"addedFromHand","type":"bool","direction":"in"}],"functions":[{"name":"ExecuteUbergraph_card_unit_somoa_s35","owner":"card_unit_somoa_s35_C","params":[{"name":"EntryPoint","type":"int32","direction":"in"}],"pure":false,"event":false,"flags":"FUNC_Final | FUNC_UbergraphFunction | FUNC_HasDefaults","locals":[{"name":"Temp_int_Loop_Counter_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"Temp_int_Array_Index_Variable","type":"int32","direction":"in"},{"name":"CallFunc_Add_IntInt_